      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm having an issue with this vba macro for our monthly reconciliation. every time i run it it gives me \"run-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\" on this line:\n\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Report\")\n    ws.Range(\"A1:Z1\").Value = \"Monthly Reconciliation Report\"\n    ws.Range(\"A1:Z1\").Font.Bold = True\n    ws.Columns(\"A:Z\").AutoFit\nEnd Sub\n\ncan u tell me why this is happening? its urgent.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the \"1004\" error, a common mischief-maker in the land of VBA, often caused by a missing or incorrect reference to a worksheet or object. In your case, it seems like the \"Recon_Report\" worksheet might not be present in your workbook. Perhaps it's a ghost sheet, vanishing into thin air, or maybe it's hiding in a different folder altogether. Make sure that the \"Recon_Report\" sheet exists in the same workbook as your macro. If it's a ghost sheet, you'll need to summon it back into existence by ensuring it's properly named and located. If it's hiding in a different folder, you'll need to navigate to its home and bring it back to the workbook. Once you've done that, your macro should run smoothly, and the \"1004\" error will be banished forever. Good luck, brave coder!",
